      "text": "To determine the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by both 3 and 6, we need to find the greatest three-digit number and check if it is divisible by both 3 and 6.\n\n1. Identify the greatest three-digit number:\n   The greatest three-digit number is \\(999\\).\n\n2. Check divisibility by 3:\n   A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. Let's check the sum of the digits of 999:\n   \\[\n   9 + 9 + 9 = 27\n   \\]\n   Since \\(27\\) is divisible by \\(3\\), \\(999\\) is divisible by \\(3\\).\n\n3. Check divisibility by 6:\n   A number is divisible by 6 if it is divisible by both 2 and 3. Since we already know that \\(999\\) is divisible by \\(3\\), we now check if it is divisible by \\(2\\).\n   The number \\(999\\) is odd, so it is not divisible by \\(2\\). Therefore, \\(999\\) is not divisible by \\(6\\).\n\nSince \\(999\\) is not divisible by \\(6\\), we need to find the next greatest three-digit number that is divisible by \\(6\\) and check if it meets the criteria for divisibility by \\(3\\).\n\n4. Find the next greatest three-digit number divisible by 6:\n   The next number after \\(999\\) that is divisible by \\(6\\) is \\(996\\). Let's check if \\(996\\) is divisible by \\(3\\):\n   \\[\n   9 + 9 + 6 = 24\n   \\]\n   Since \\(24\\) is divisible by \\(3\\), \\(996\\) is divisible by \\(3\\).\n\n5. Check divisibility by 6:\n   Since \\(996\\) is also divisible by \\(6\\) (as it is divisible by both \\(2\\) and \\(3\\)), it satisfies both conditions.\n\nTherefore, the greatest possible three-digit number that is divisible by 3 and divisible by 6 is \\(\\boxed{996}\\).",
